---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Closing from "../components/Closing.astro";
import CompareTable from "../components/CompareTable.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Feature from "../components/Feature.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Languages from "../components/Languages.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import QuickStart from "../components/QuickStart.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import CompetingOptions from "../components/demos/CompetingOptions.astro";
import DocsTiles from "../components/demos/DocsTiles.astro";
import DrillDown from "../components/demos/DrillDown.astro";
import EvidenceTabs from "../components/demos/EvidenceTabs.astro";
import Grammars from "../components/demos/Grammars.astro";
import HistoryTimeline from "../components/demos/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import Measures from "../components/demos/Measures.astro";
import OwnModel from "../components/demos/OwnModel.astro";
import Pipeline from "../components/demos/Pipeline.astro";
import ProposalSwitch from "../components/demos/ProposalSwitch.astro";
import TakeBack from "../components/demos/TakeBack.astro";
import WalkthroughPlayer from "../components/demos/WalkthroughPlayer.astro";
import { links } from "../lib/links";
---

<Base
	title="Archboard: architecture your coding agent keeps current"
	description="Your coding agent reads the code and tells Archboard what the system is. Archboard draws every line, draws each proposed change against what is built, and keeps the reason for every change."
>
	<Nav />
	<main id="main">
		<Hero />
		<Languages />

		<Section id="how" title="The agent writes meaning.<br />Archboard draws.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Your agent states what the system is: its services, stores and queues, what contains what,
					and how they talk, in what order. No file stores a box position, so no diagram decays into
					a hand-tuned layout.
				</p>
				<p>
					The agent that changes the code updates the map in the same task. A renderer fix redraws
					every reading, history included.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<Pipeline />
		</Section>

		<Section id="understand" title="Read a system you did not write.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Archboard joins the model to the code and to its Git history. See where the design costs the
					most before anyone changes it.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<Feature title="Find the smells in your design" wide>
				<p>
					John Ousterhout's <em>A Philosophy of Software Design</em> names the signs of costly design:
					shallow modules, pass-through methods, information leakage and change amplification. Measures
					lays out the facts behind each sign. Sort by any column.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Many entries over few hidden parts suggests a shallow module.</li>
					<li>Unjoined counts modules that change together with no relationship between them.</li>
					<li>There is no score. You and your agent read the code and judge.</li>
				</ul>
				<Measures slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<EvidenceTabs />
		</Section>

		<Section id="plan" title="See the change before anyone builds it.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Ask your agent for a change and you get a proposal, drawn against what exists. New modules
					arrive green, changed ones orange, and removed ones stay on the page as red ghosts.
				</p>
				<p>Current architecture stays as it is until the proposal ships.</p>
			</Fragment>

			<div class="plan-stage" data-reveal>
				<ProposalSwitch />
				<p class="caption">
					The example shop's Queued fulfilment proposal: checkout answers once the payment starts, and a
					worker reserves the stock and sends the receipt. The counts are the answer of
					<code>archboard model compare</code>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="options-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Weigh competing options side by side</h3>
				<p class="body">
					Branch two drafts from one starting point and open them in two panes. Both compare against the
					same root, and neither touches current architecture.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="options-wrap">
				<CompetingOptions />
			</div>

			<Feature title="Keep a record that remembers why" flip>
				<p>
					When a proposal ships, your agent adopts it and records the reason. The architecture it
					replaces stays available as an exact checkpoint, and a shelved proposal keeps its reason too.
				</p>
				<p>
					Retained history lives in a separate Git archive, published only when you choose. Module files,
					flows, walkthroughs and active proposals stay in the repository for code review.
				</p>
				<HistoryTimeline slot="demo" />
			</Feature>
		</Section>

		<Section id="present" title="Let the architecture explain itself.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					A walkthrough is an authored tour through readings. Press Narrate and the Codex voice explains
					each step while the camera follows the selected parts. One command films the tour for a design
					review or a pull request.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<div class="walk" data-reveal>
				<WalkthroughPlayer />
				<div class="walk-foot">
					<p class="caption">
						The example shop's own walkthrough, “How an order is placed today”. In Archboard the Codex
						voice speaks each step. Here the
						words play without sound. <a href={links.walkthroughs}>Hear a narrated pull-request video</a>.
					</p>
					<pre class="walk-cmd mono"><span class="ps">$</span> archboard model record --checkpoint &lt;id&gt; --walkthrough "Why checkout stops waiting" --out pr-128.mp4</pre>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Feature title="One model, every kind of diagram" wide>
				<p>
					Ask what happens as a shopper pays, and get a sequence diagram: who calls whom, what comes back,
					which call retries and how often. Your agent traces each message to the code that sends it.
				</p>
				<p>
					The sequence and the flowchart use the same module identities as the architecture, and a
					proposal that changes an exchange shows which messages changed.
				</p>
				<Grammars slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Zoom from the system into a service" flip>
				<p>
					Open a module to see its parts and the connections that cross its boundary. Open a part to go
					deeper. Breadcrumbs lead back through the same model, and nothing is copied on the way.
				</p>
				<p>Select the Inventory service card to fly into it.</p>
				<DrillDown slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Agents work in plain sight">
				<p>
					An agent claims the subjects it plans to change. Each pane shows the writer, the purpose, the
					subjects and the start time.
				</p>
				<p>
					Take back control ends the claim, and the server refuses the former intent. Every write names
					the exact revision it read, so a stale write is refused too.
				</p>
				<TakeBack slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<div class="tiles-wrap">
				<DocsTiles />
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="self" title="Archboard draws itself.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Archboard keeps its own architecture in its repository and changes it through proposals, with
					the same commands a consumer uses.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<div data-reveal>
				<OwnModel />
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="compare" title="Next to the tools you know.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Diagram tools ask you to write the diagram in text or to draw it by hand, and then it drifts
					from the code. Archboard asks your agent what the system is, checks it against the code, and
					keeps every change with its reason.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<CompareTable />
		</Section>

		<Section id="quick-start" title="Run it from a clone.">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Archboard runs locally. The server binds to 127.0.0.1, and rendering, repository access and the
					agent stay on your machine.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<QuickStart />
		</Section>

		<Section id="faq" title="Questions.">
			<Faq />
		</Section>

		<Closing />
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Base>

<style>
	.plan-stage {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
	}
	.plan-stage .caption,
	.walk-foot .caption {
		max-width: 52em;
	}
	.caption code {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	.options-head {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
		align-items: end;
		gap: 16px 96px;
		margin: clamp(88px, 10vw, 140px) 0 32px;
	}
	.options-wrap {
		margin-bottom: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.walk {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
		margin-bottom: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.walk-foot {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 12px 32px;
	}
	.walk-cmd {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--ink-muted);
		overflow-x: auto;
		max-width: 100%;
	}
	.ps {
		color: var(--gold-mark);
	}
	.tiles-wrap {
		margin-top: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	@media (max-width: 900px) {
		.options-head {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
